    - measurement_0006  # 6ea3
    isbefore: []  # 6ea3
    duplicate_of: []  # 6ea3
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 6ea3
    pr_ref:  # 6ea3
    description: "Found by the 0006 adversarial refutation pass (docs/measurements/0006-taint-precision-under-the-ratified-frame.md), reported independently by 6 of 14 refuters over 5 languages.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A NAMING NIT: a finding can be TRUE VALUE-FLOW and VACUOUS AS A CLAIM. All five shellcheck true positives in 0006 are real dataflow filed under untrusted-input-no-database against Haskell in-process refs. The rubric scores the value chain, so these count as TP -- which makes 0006 headline precision (33.9%/situation) an UPPER BOUND on useful precision rather than a measure of it. Any precision number computed without a semantic check on the primitive kind inherits this.\n\nRELATED BUT DISTINCT, cite do not fold: INV-fodar (io_boundary and taint evaluate qualified-exact vs module-filter in OPPOSITE directions) is about HOW a name is matched to a catalogue row; this item is about the row asserting a boundary KIND that the primitive does not have. INV-sukoh (one env read matches two rows) is about multiplicity. WI-sutuk / WI-bahid (third-party rows shipping in catalogues) is about ROW PROVENANCE.\n\nNOT YET DONE: no fix is proposed here and the per-language row counts are not enumerated. The five instances above are the ones 0006 happened to sample; the catalogue has not been swept for others."  # 6ea3
    fields:  # 6ea3
      statement: "A taint source/sink primitive is credited by NAME alone; its declared boundary KIND is never checked against what the primitive actually does. Measured on 0006 across five languages: haskell IORef/STRef declared db_read (in-process mutable cells, and newIORef is a CONSTRUCTOR that writes and reads nothing); c socket declared net_recv (returns a descriptor, receives nothing -- the receive is recvmsg); python parse_args declared env_read (reads argv, not environ); elixir Application.get_env declared env_read (application config, not the process environment); bash > declared fs_write with no redirect-TARGET check, so >&2 and >/dev/null both count, and >> is reported as >, losing append/truncate; bash BASH_SOURCE declared env_read (bash-internal, never exported)."  # 6ea3
      root_cause: "io_primitives rows bind a name to a boundary kind with no verification that the named primitive performs that boundary operation; the matcher then credits any name hit."  # 6ea3
- op: discuss  # b8ee
  at: '2026-08-26T03:07:42Z'  # b8ee
  by: agent  # b8ee
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b8ee
  clock: 2  # b8ee
  nonce: b8ee  # b8ee
  message: "BASH HALF ADDRESSED. Two of the six reported bash claims DO NOT REPRODUCE, and\nthe item's statement should be read with that correction.\n\nREPRODUCED AND FIXED\n  1. `> /dev/null` counted as a host filesystem write. Behavioral, shipped CLI,\n     control in the same run, claim {boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true}:\n       /tmp/nul2  echo \"$API_KEY\" > /dev/null      violated rc 1 -> confirmed rc 0\n       /tmp/nul3  echo \"$API_KEY\" > /etc/cron.d/..  violated rc 1 -> violated rc 1\n     Fix: bash.py stamps io_target_kind (host_path / null_device / std_stream /\n     unresolved); io_boundary.target_kinds_cross_no_boundary refuses the\n     boundary when EVERY collapsed call site discards. `> \"$OUT\"` stays\n     classified -- \"wrote somewhere I cannot name\" is a real write.\n  2. Bash-maintained variables counted as environment reads, deriving\n     host_secret. Both arms measured in process ($HOSTNAME, $BASH_SOURCE):\n       ARM A (pre-fix):  ['bash:env:0-0:env.environ:attribute']      -> host_secret\n       ARM B (post-fix): ['bash:shell:0-0:shell.hostinfo:attribute'] -> host_description\n                         ($BASH_SOURCE emits nothing)\n     End to end: $HOSTNAME -> /tmp/out.txt now CONFIRMS host-secret-no-host-fs,\n     while $API_KEY -> /tmp/out.txt still VIOLATES it (control).\n\nDID NOT REPRODUCE -- do not carry these forward\n  3. \">&2 counts\". It does not. _redirect_edge returns None for fd duplication;\n     `echo hi >&2` emits NO edge. That is a RECALL gap (a write to stderr is a\n     real logging crossing bash does not report) -- the OPPOSITE of the\n     over-report filed. Not fixed here; not this item.\n  4. \">> is reported as >, losing append/truncate\". It is not. The operators\n     carry distinct dst ids (redirect:0-0:>> vs redirect:0-0:>) and distinct\n     io_mode (a vs w), end to end into the io-boundaries map, and\n     test_bash_redirection.py has pinned it since INV-vavup.\n\nTHE RULE DRAWN, because \"which names are secrets\" would be the curated list the\nenv_read row exists to refuse: WHO SET THE VARIABLE. INV-jurif's own\ndiscriminator one level out -- a name the SCRIPT assigns is not an env read\n(shipped), and neither is a name BASH assigns. $HOME stays an env read; bash\ninherits it rather than setting it. Among bash-assigned names, the 8 that\ndescribe host or user go to host_info_read on INV-tutar's precedent (python\nalready files getcwd/getuid/uname there); the other 50 cross no boundary.\n\nA WRONG FIRST CUT, RECORDED: refusing the match in classify_call_in_catalog --\nwhere the coverage gate also reads it -- turned the false `violated` into\n`inconclusive` rc 2 (\"calls into 1 module(s) that the I/O catalog could not\nclassify (redirect)\"). EXAMINED and CROSSES are different questions of the same\nmatch. Moved to the tagger; a regression test pins that a discarding call is\nstill classified.\n\nSTILL OPEN ON THIS ITEM (not closed by this):\n  - haskell IORef/STRef/MVar under db_read/db_write. Note INV-vaduk shape 4\n    already names this exact question and calls it \"a vocabulary decision\n    beyond this row\"; the STM rows carry boundary_ruling: unruled about it.\n  - c sys/socket socket/bind/listen under net_recv (socket() returns a\n    descriptor and receives nothing).\n  - python argparse.parse_args and elixir Application.get_env under env_read.\n    Both are DOCUMENTED deliberate choices in their rows, not oversights --\n    argv is consistent with the sys.argv row beside it, and Application config\n    is an ambient configuration read. These need a vocabulary ruling, not a\n    row edit, and lumping them with the bash defects would misstate them.\n  - /dev/stderr is MARKED (std_stream) and not reclassified. That write really\n    does leave the process; calling it `logging` needs the logging-vs-fs_write\n    decision the haskell hPutStrLn rows already wait on (INV-vaduk shape 4).\n  - The catalogue has still not been SWEPT for other kind-vs-semantics\n    mismatches; the instances above are the ones 0006 happened to sample.\n  - SEPARATE DEFECT FOUND IN PASSING: c.yaml had DUPLICATE `notes:` keys in two\n    rows (net_send unistd.write, net_recv unistd.read), so PyYAML silently kept\n    the second and the INV-vaduk boundary_ruling rationale was discarded. A\n    sweep of all 21 catalogue/overlay YAMLs found exactly these two. Not fixed\n    in this PR -- it belongs with the c rows above."  # b8ee
