  clock: 11  # 5403
  nonce: '5403'  # 5403
  message: "HASKELL INSTANCE FOUND, FILED AS WI-joruz. Adds to this item's own\n\"the catalogue has not been swept for others\".\n\nNetwork.Wai.responseLBS / responseFile / responseBuilder / responseStream are\ncatalogued net_recv. They are RESPONSE CONSTRUCTORS -- they build the payload a\nserver is about to send -- so each mints untrusted_input via\nAUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP at a call that observes nothing. That is the F5 mechanism\nin a language F5 never touched: #609 fixed exactly this for 26 Phoenix/Plug\nhelpers in elixir, and the Haskell spelling was missed because the sweep ran\nPER-CATALOGUE rather than per-MECHANISM.\n\nThe methodological point is the part worth keeping on this item rather than\nonly on WI-joruz: the six families were enumerated by walking catalogues, and a\nmechanism that recurs across languages is invisible to that traversal order. A\nper-mechanism sweep -- \"every row whose name is a response/render/reply verb,\nin every catalogue\" -- would have caught elixir and haskell in one pass. The\nremaining ~2889 unadjudicated single-boundary rows should be stratified that\nway at least once before this item's coverage claim is believed.\n\nFound while relocating haskell's third-party rows for WI-surun, and NOT fixed\nthere: WI-surun changes where a row lives, not what it claims, and folding a\nboundary correction into a relocation would have made that PR's A/B measure two\nchanges at once."  # 5403
- op: discuss  # ba87
  at: '2026-08-31T00:15:28Z'  # ba87
  by: agent  # ba87
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ba87
  clock: 12  # ba87
  nonce: ba87  # ba87
  message: "F6 ADDED: ADJACENCY IS NOT A CROSSING. A fourth family, distinct from F1-F3 in\nmechanism rather than in degree.\n\nF1-F3 rows misdescribe what a call DOES -- a ref is not a database, a builder\nissues no statement. F6 rows describe the call CORRECTLY and then declare a\nboundary for a different call one hop away, on the grounds that a real crossing\nusually happens nearby. The crossing is real; it is attributed to the wrong\ncall. ADR-0049 ruling 1 pointed outbound is the test.\n\nMEMBERS FOUND, by a derived gate rather than by enumeration:\n  erlang + elixir  io_lib.{format,fwrite} under logging  -- REMOVED (this PR)\n  javascript       seven path.* rows under fs_read       -- WI-tunod, unfixed\n  python           urllib.request.Request under net_send -- WI-gihif, unfixed\n\nThe two unfixed ones are unfixed on purpose: ADR-0049 ruling 3's proof is run\nPER LANGUAGE and not argued, and neither has had its run. javascript's prior is\nalso materially different -- fs.readFile CONSUMES a joined path as an argument\nrather than being reached from it, so removal there could delete a crossing\nrather than relocate one.\n\nTHE GATE IS DERIVED, which is the part worth keeping. Pinning io_lib would\ncatch io_lib and nothing else. A general sweep for self-refuting notes (\"not\nI/O\", \"returns\", \"constructor\") returned 13 hits of which 11 were notes\nNARRATING A PAST REMOVAL -- including several of this item's own F1/F2 notes --\nso that gate would have needed an 11-row exemption list and would have rotted.\nMatching the RATIONALISATION instead (\"typically|commonly|usually|often\" near\n\"precede|follow|immediately|written\") returns exactly the live families across\nthree languages, and is what found javascript's path.* rows without anyone\nlooking for them.\n\nEVIDENCE FOR THE erlang REMOVAL, at the finding level per ruling 3, both arms\ncold on separate XDG_CACHE_HOME with the cold line asserted (the survey cache\nkeys on repo CONTENT, so a shared cache would have produced a clean fake null):\nrabbitmq host-secret-no-logging stays VIOLATED in both arms, 100 -> 68 flows;\nsink modules io_lib 94 -> 0 and io 5 -> 64; all three of measurement 0006's\nadjudicated true positives re-root onto io:format; the other six claims have\nbyte-identical evidence sets.\n\nTHIS CORRECTS MEASUREMENT 0006. Its sensitivity S1 projected useful precision\nat 21.4% ON THE ASSUMPTION the three TPs would vanish. They do not, so nothing\nis deducted and useful precision is unchanged at 24.1%. 0006 named the same\nshape for urllib.request.Request two paragraphs later and called it \"an\nattribution defect, not a vacuous claim\" -- it filed two identical shapes into\ndifferent buckets because the rule was never stated. Stated now, in the 0006\ncorrection: a row that misattributes WHERE a crossing happens is an attribution\ndefect; only a row asserting a crossing that does not happen at all is vacuous.\n\nA WIDER HOLE UNDERNEATH, filed as INV-zihor (P1) rather than absorbed here:\nsome flows that vanished with the row were REAL -- do_http_req/2 logs an\nenv-derived timeout -- but via ?LOG_DEBUG, an OTP macro the analyzer never\nexpands. rabbitmq has 1,910 ?LOG_* uses against 187 direct logger: calls, so\n91% of its logging surface is invisible and io_lib:format was covering it BY\nCOINCIDENCE. Keeping a wrong row to stand in for a missing one would make the\ncorrect fix look like a regression."  # ba87
- op: discuss  # 1bd6
  at: '2026-08-31T01:23:45Z'  # 1bd6
  by: agent  # 1bd6
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1bd6
  clock: 13  # 1bd6
  nonce: 1bd6  # 1bd6
  message: "CORRECTION TO THE F6 ENTRY ABOVE, 2026-08-30. A blind panel refuted the\nmeasurement claim I filed with the io_lib removal. Recorded here rather than\nedited into the earlier entry, because the error is the useful part.\n\nWHAT I CLAIMED: that measurement 0006's three adjudicated rabbitmq true\npositives \"re-root onto io:format\", so ADR-0049 ruling 3's represented-crossing\ntest was met, S1 was void as a deduction, and useful precision was unchanged at\n24.1%.\n\nWHAT THE PANEL FOUND: two independent blind passes, given the flow and the\nsource but not the prior labels and walled off from every ledger, DISAGREEMENT\n0 OF 7. All four rabbitmq situations are FALSE POSITIVES. Both passes derived\nthe same structural refutation without conferring: the re-rooted route\nterminates at rabbit_misc:module_attributes/1, whose io:format\n(rabbit_misc.erl:731) has exactly one argument list -- [Module] -- bound at\n:755-762 from `{ok, Modules} <- [application:get_key(App, modules)]`. Neither\nparameter of module_attributes_from_apps/2 reaches it: one is used only in an\nequality test (control dependence), the other only selects which external table\nto read (resource selection, excluded by the rubric's tie-break). Each panellist\nthen searched independently for any real chain and found none; the real\nformatting runs through rabbit_misc:format/2 = lists:flatten(io_lib:format(...))\nand is handed to ?LOG_* macros, which are logger:.\n\nSO THE REMOVAL DID THE REVERSE OF WHAT I SAID. It replaced a VACUOUS TRUE\nPOSITIVE -- the value genuinely reaches io_lib:format, which writes nothing --\nwith an OUTRIGHT FALSE POSITIVE. S1's deduction STANDS; useful precision under\nit is 21.4% (24/112), the figure 0006 published in the first place.\n\nNOT RETRACTED: io_lib:format returns an iolist and writes nothing, the owner's\nruling against the row, or the removal itself -- it deleted vacuous findings,\nwhich is the right outcome. The derived adjacency gate and the javascript\npath.* finding (WI-tunod) are untouched. Only the LICENCE ARGUMENT is\nwithdrawn: for these three situations there is no real crossing to represent.\n\nTHE LESSON, which generalises past this row. The A/B was run correctly --\nboth arms cold on separate XDG_CACHE_HOME with the cold line asserted, a clean\nsix-claim control, catalogue restored and verified by sha. What an A/B over\nTOOL OUTPUT cannot do is establish that a finding is TRUE. PRESENCE OF A\nFINDING AFTER A CHANGE IS NOT EVIDENCE THE FINDING IS CORRECT, and re-rooting\nis not relocation unless the destination chain is real. An A/B prices a CHANGE;\nonly adjudication prices a CLAIM. 0006's own headline exists because its pass 4\nrefuted 7 of 44 panel-agreed true positives -- the same discipline one level\ndown would have caught this before it shipped."  # 1bd6
- op: discuss  # 1676
  at: '2026-09-03T00:25:37Z'  # 1676
  by: agent  # 1676
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1676
  clock: 14  # 1676
  nonce: '1676'  # 1676
  message: "TWO NEW MEMBERS, from measurement 0012's ADR-0046 vacuity pass. Both are the\nMISSING-boundary_ruling shape rather than a wrong kind, which makes them\nsiblings of the c `socket` instance already in the statement.\n\n1. go.yaml:101 declares io.WriteString under fs_write with NO boundary_ruling.\n   Its first argument is an io.Writer, so the boundary is decided by the\n   WRITER, not the call. gocryptfs:untrusted-input-no-host-fs:0 writes to\n   cmd.StdinPipe() -- a pipe to a child process -- and was reported as a\n   host-filesystem crossing. Deducted VACUOUS:KIND-MISDECLARED.\n\n2. c.yaml:90 declares sys/socket.sendmsg under net_send with NO\n   boundary_ruling, while the VERY NEXT ROW gives unistd.write one for exactly\n   this reason (\"exactly one of fs_write/ipc_send/net_send is true per call\").\n   tmux:host-description-no-network:6 sends over an fd that is always AF_UNIX\n   (client.c:122, server.c:123, proc.c:364 socketpair) -- process-local IPC,\n   the catalogue's own ipc_send zone. tmux opens no AF_INET socket anywhere.\n   Deducted VACUOUS:KIND-MISDECLARED.\n\nTHE CONTRAST WORTH KEEPING: haskell.yaml's hPutStrLn row DOES carry\nboundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable and the note \"stderr reads as logging, a\nfile handle as fs_write\". shellcheck:host-secret-no-host-fs:2 was deducted on\nthe strength of the catalogue's own declaration -- the row told the adjudicator\nit was undecidable and the call site resolved it. Where the declaration is\npresent the deduction is mechanical; where it is absent (go, c) the adjudicator\nhas to notice. So this is not only a correctness gap, it is a gap in what the\ncatalogue can be used to CHECK.\n\nThese three are the entire VACUOUS class of 0012 (3 of 32 correctness TPs).\nFixing them would take useful precision from 30.9% to 34.0%, i.e. the whole\nremaining gap between the two figures on this population is INV-nular.\n\nFull derivation: docs/measurements/0012-taint-precision-after-escape-closure.md"  # 1676
- op: discuss  # ba6a
  at: '2026-09-03T09:05:26Z'  # ba6a
  by: agent  # ba6a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ba6a
  clock: 15  # ba6a
  nonce: ba6a  # ba6a
  message: "THE 09-03 VACUOUS CLASS NOW HAS OWNERS, filed 2026-09-03 after sizing rather than absorbed here.\n\n  WI-suhug (P1)  go: the WRITER decides io.WriteString / fmt.Fprint* -- the write-side twin of INV-bagok / WI-vutav. Sized on six Go repos: 6,373 writer-argument calls (Fprintf 2,897, io.Copy 2,537, Fprintln 529, Fprint 293, WriteString 117); first argument os.Stdout/err 1,695, an in-memory buffer by address 2,637, a bare identifier 1,620. Design filed on the item: write_boundary_for_target_kind, the seam generalised from 'two READ boundaries' to 'two boundaries of one direction', TaintSink.requires_target_kind, rows dual under call_site_undecidable with abstains_to = today's answer, the stamp from argument 0 at the existing site-C wrapper stamp, and two NEW vocabulary values -- `pipe` -> ipc_send and `net_stream` -> net_send / net_recv.\n  WI-baran (P2)  c: sys/socket send*/recv* by socket FAMILY from the fd's binding (socketpair / AF_UNIX -> process-local; AF_INET -> network). Depends on WI-suhug's vocabulary.\n\nTWO THINGS THE RECORD SAYS THAT SHAPED THE DESIGN. (1) INV-bagok's later ruling -- do not declare a row no stamp can select -- outranks INV-vaduk's triple-declared unistd.write precedent, so the go rows land WITH the write-side seam, not before it. (2) A COLLISION, resolved by the later cross-language ruling and disclosed rather than silently followed: c.yaml's unistd.write ipc_send row says 'write(STDOUT_FILENO, ...)' is an IPC send, while WI-dutah (shipped 05-13 for c/rust/js/elixir) rules terminal output `logging`. std_stream WRITE -> logging follows WI-dutah; that c.yaml note is the stale sibling.\n\nverify_claims.py and taint.py have NO consumer of boundary_ruling (grep: none), so the declaration alone would make the 0012 deduction mechanical for a human adjudicator and change no verdict; the seam is what moves the 30.9% -> 34.0%."  # ba6a
